
<main class="dui-story-page first-dui-page"
      style="--dui-desktop: url('<%= asset_path('addiction_first_dui_desktop.png') %>'); --dui-mobile: url('<%= asset_path('addiction_first_dui_mobile.png') %>');">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="ds-hero">
    <div class="ds-hero-inner">

      <p class="ds-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / PERSPECTIVES / FIRST DUI
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>FIRST</span>
        <span>DUI.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="ds-intro">
        THE PAIN.<br>
        THE RELIEF.<br>
        THE CONSEQUENCES.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- CONTEXT -->
  <section class="ds-context">
    <p>
      Chronic pain. Alcohol. A blackout. A totaled car.
      A missing dog. This is the story of my first DUI,
      told through what I remember and what I learned afterward.
    </p>
  </section>


  <!-- STORY -->
  <section class="ds-body">
    <div class="ds-layout">

      <aside class="ds-sidebar">

        <p class="ds-label">01 / MY ACCOUNT</p>

        <h2>
          IT STARTED<br>
          WITH THE<br>
          PAIN.
        </h2>

        <div class="ds-note">
          SCIATICA / ALCOHOL / BLACKOUT / CONSEQUENCES
        </div>

      </aside>


      <article class="ds-copy" aria-label="My first DUI story">

        <!-- BEFORE IT HAPPENED -->
        <h2>THE PAIN.</h2>

        <p>
          It was another day in Mary’s basement. My left
          leg was screaming in pain, my back was stiff
          and ached. I had never experienced pain like
          this in my entire life. Having just moved to
          Colorado, I was waiting for my health insurance
          to kick in and receive a letter in the mail
          from my new insurance company—and the mailbox
          was again empty today.
        </p>

        <p>
          Despite the pain pulsing through my body, I
          miraculously felt well enough to sit up if
          I was in the right position, so I decided
          to take the dog for a drive and go see the city.
          The first part of the drive was uneventful
          enough until I saw someone more than twice
          my age pedal past my car at a red light on
          a bicycle, and I ended up stopping at the
          first liquor store I passed.
        </p>


        <!-- THE DECISION -->
        <h2>THE RELIEF.</h2>

        <p class="ds-impact">
          I began to drink while I drove.
        </p>

        <p>
          Drinking eased the pain, and it was my only
          “solution” I had come up with to relieve
          the pain while I waited to see a doctor and
          get medical care. I was also seeing a
          chiropractor, but he was expensive and it
          wasn’t providing relief like a steroid would.
          I could feel the relief that the alcohol
          provided, so that was the reason I drank
          more and faster; I was seeking quicker,
          additional, expeditated relief.
        </p>


        <!-- THE BLACKOUT -->
        <div class="ds-blackout">
          <span class="ds-blackout-label">
            THE LAST THING I REMEMBER
          </span>

          <p>
            THAT’S ALL<br>
            I REMEMBER.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- POLICE -->
        <h2>THE NEXT THING.</h2>

        <p>
          Next thing, I was sitting in a police station
          slurring my words, blowing into a breathalyzer
          and filling out paperwork. I learned they
          appreciate it when you are nice to them and
          give you special privileges. But you still
          go to jail, just with nice treatment.
          They will take off the cuffs.
        </p>

        <div class="ds-jail-commands">
          <p>SURRENDER YOUR PHONE.</p>
          <p>BEND AND COUGH.</p>
          <p>PUT ON THESE ORANGE CLOTHES.</p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Surrender your phone. Bend and cough.
          Put on these orange clothes. I spent
          the night in Denver County. Next day
          I was bailed out.
        </p>


        <!-- AFTERMATH -->
        <h2>THE AFTERMATH.</h2>

        <p class="ds-impact">
          That was the beginning of my troubles.
        </p>

        <div class="ds-consequences">
          <span>THE DOG WAS MISSING.</span>
          <span>MY CAR WAS TOTALED.</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          The dog was missing. My car was totaled.
          I was completely ashamed, and I learned
          I had crashed into a parked trailer.
          My mind was absolutely spinning out of
          control with worst case scenarios,
          especially about where Banjo was.
          He was eventually located at the local
          shelter, leash still on him and
          completely neglected as it looked.
        </p>


        <!-- COURT -->
        <h2>THE CONSEQUENCES.</h2>

        <p>
          I had my day in court, and I plead guilty.
          I ended up with four weeks house arrest,
          community service, alcohol treatment
          classes and therapy, drug testing,
          and community service. It was doable,
          and I completed it all.
        </p>

        <p class="ds-story-final">
          I COMPLETED IT ALL.
        </p>

      </article>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="ds-ending">
    <div class="ds-ending-inner">

      <p class="ds-ending-eyebrow">
        FIRST DUI / THE STORY
      </p>

      <h2>
        THE STORY.<br>
        THEN THE LENSES.
      </h2>

      <p class="ds-ending-copy">
        The circumstances explain what led to the drinking.
        They don't erase the consequences. The perspectives
        examine suffering, relief, decisions, responsibility,
        and what happened afterward.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION PERSPECTIVES",
          addiction_perspectives_path,
          class: "ds-back" %>

    </div>
  </section>

</main>
